### Did the Joe Biden 2020 presidential campaign issue any official clarifications regarding Joe Biden's stance on hydraulic fracturing (fracking) following Joe Biden's debate remarks?
Answer: Yes, following the debate, Joe Biden's campaign clarified his remarks by stating that he was "simply restating his own plan to ban new oil and gas drilling on federal lands" rather than proposing a complete ban on fracking.

Source URL: https://www.chron.com/business/energy/article/biden-fracking-bernie-pledge-ban-debate-15133916.php

### Does a United States President have the executive authority to unilaterally ban hydraulic fracturing (fracking) on private lands?
Answer: No, a United States President does not have the executive authority to unilaterally ban fracking on private lands. According to the search result, "the president wouldn’t have the executive power unless Congress passed a new law," as existing federal environmental statutes do not allow the executive branch to set absolute limits on production. While a president can use federal agencies like the EPA to regulate the environmental impacts of fracking, banning the practice entirely on private and state lands—where roughly 90 percent of fracking occurs—would require legislative action from Congress.

Source URL: https://thebulletin.org/2020/10/about-last-nights-fracking-comments-in-the-debate/

### What was the official position of the Joe Biden 2020 presidential campaign on the transition away from fossil fuels as of October 2020?
Answer: As of October 2020, the official position of the Joe Biden presidential campaign was to transition the country away from fossil fuels to achieve net-zero emissions by the year 2050, "by shifting away from fossil fuels but not completely banning them." Instead of an outright ban, Biden's policy focused on ending federal subsidies for the fossil fuel industry, developing carbon capture technology, and banning new fracking permits exclusively on federal land. Biden clarified this stance following a debate, stating, "We’re not getting rid of fossil fuels. We’re getting rid of the subsidies of fossil fuels, but we’re not getting rid of fossil fuels for a long time."

