### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence indicates that during the 2020 presidential campaign, Joe Biden's official policy platform did not propose a complete nationwide ban on fracking, and both he and Kamala Harris repeatedly asserted that he would not ban the practice. Although Biden made some confusing remarks during primary debates—such as stating he would ensure fracking is "eliminated" or that there would be "no new fracking"—his campaign consistently clarified that his actual policy was strictly limited to banning new oil and gas leases on federal lands. Because approximately 90% of fracking takes place on private and state lands, and because a president lacks the unilateral executive authority to ban fracking on non-federal lands without Congress, Biden's proposed restriction was highly specific rather than a blanket ban. 

Since the retrieved evidence is clear and comprehensive, no further information is required to finalize this fact-check.

### Conclusion
Based on the documented evidence, the claim that "Joe Biden wants to ban fracking" is true only under specific conditions but is highly misleading without context. While Biden did propose a ban on new fracking permits on federal lands, he repeatedly clarified that he did not support a complete, nationwide ban on fracking, and his platform allowed existing fracking on private and state lands to continue. By omitting this crucial distinction, the claim falsely implies a total ban on the practice. Therefore, because the claim is true under certain conditions but not universally true, the final decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is classified as **cherry-picking** because it misleads by omitting crucial context regarding Joe Biden's actual policy position. While Biden made confusing statements during the Democratic primary debates suggesting he would "eliminate" fracking ([CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/23/politics/biden-fracking-fact-check/index.html)), his official 2020 campaign platform maintained that he would not ban the practice nationwide ([Newsweek](https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291)). Instead, Biden and his running mate, Kamala Harris, repeatedly clarified that he only supported banning new oil and gas permits on federal lands ([Houston Chronicle](https://www.chron.com/business/energy/article/biden-fracking-bernie-pledge-ban-debate-15133916.php); [API](https://www.api.org/news-policy-and-issues/blog/2020/10/09/joe-biden-will-not-ban-fracking)), while allowing fracking to continue on private and state lands where roughly 90% of fracking occurs ([The Bulletin](https://thebulletin.org/2020/10/about-last-nights-fracking-comments-in-the-debate/)). Furthermore, a U.S. President lacks the unilateral executive authority to ban fracking on private lands without congressional legislation ([The Bulletin](https://thebulletin.org/2020/10/about-last-nights-fracking-comments-in-the-debate/)). Thus, the claim selectively uses his debate remarks to imply a total ban, ignoring his explicit policy to only restrict new permits on federal lands ([EnergyNow](https://energynow.com/2020/09/the-biden-flip-flop-u-s-presidential-hopeful-biden-now-says-he-would-not-ban-fracking/); [ABC News](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/biden-campaign-seeks-clarify-position-fossil-fuels-debate/story?id=73789793)).